import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as d}from"./chunks/framework.k9nOhppk.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Decision log","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Decision log"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"design/decision-log.md","filePath":"design/decision-log.md","lastUpdated":1791864160000}'),s={name:"design/decision-log.md"};function n(r,e,c,i,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="decision-log" tabindex="-1">Decision log <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision-log" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision log&quot;">​</a></h1><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Decision</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Core model</td><td><strong>Entities</strong> hold shared data; <strong>jobs</strong> are durable functions; <strong>views</strong> react to conditions across many entities</td><td>Data has a home, waiting has a home, and cross-entity conditions don&#39;t need polling</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting</td><td><strong>Only jobs wait.</strong> Entity methods are short atomic transactions</td><td>No state changes in the middle of a method; every method is all-or-nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Transactions</td><td><strong>Entity methods and served operations stay inside the program</strong>: they change state, call pure code and other entities&#39; methods, start jobs and publish events, which happen only when the transaction commits, and they can&#39;t use <code>net</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>process</code>, <code>clock</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>ffi</code>, <code>secret</code> or <code>wait</code>. Output (<code>print</code>, <code>log</code>) is allowed (<a href="/reference/language/entities">section 14</a>)</td><td>The runtime may run a transaction again after a conflict, so a call to a service or a file write in one would happen twice, which the keyed calls of promise 4 (<a href="/design/goals">section 1</a>) exist to prevent; what commits with the transaction happens once. A line of output written twice harms no one. The clock is outside too: a method takes the time from its caller, so a retry sees the same time. From the spec review of 2026-10-12: methods could call services and sleep</td></tr><tr><td>Wait marker</td><td>A <strong><code>!</code> suffix</strong> on every durable step in a job, which is every call that may pause or has an outside effect: <code>sleep!(20 min)</code>, <code>notify.send!(…)</code></td><td>Every save point is visible in code review</td></tr><tr><td>Checkpoints</td><td>Every <code>!</code> call in a job is a recorded <strong>step</strong>: its result is saved and it runs once. A <strong>checkpoint</strong> is a run&#39;s saved place, its live variables and statement, taken at the statements of the job&#39;s body once steps have been taken; a run resumes from its latest and replays only the steps since (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>)</td><td>No separate <code>step</code> keyword is needed</td></tr><tr><td>Entity lifecycle</td><td><strong>Virtual</strong>: an entity exists on first use. Ending one with protected state needs an <code>Approval</code></td><td>No create/exists dance; deletion always involves a human</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>An <strong>embeddable engine</strong> with backends behind a <code>Store</code> trait. The default backend keeps everything in memory and is written in Eyelang (<code>check/runtime/store.eye</code>); a journal file wraps it for durability on one machine (open question 9); <strong>Postgres</strong> is selectable</td><td>Programs, tests and embedders need no database; production picks durability; writing the default backend in Eyelang tests the language on real systems code</td></tr><tr><td>Types</td><td><strong>Static.</strong> Public items carry full signatures, including effects; everything inside bodies is inferred</td><td>Changes to types and permissions show up in the diff</td></tr><tr><td>Errors</td><td><strong>Result only</strong>: no exceptions. <code>?</code> wraps an error into the caller&#39;s error type when exactly one of its variants holds it; otherwise <code>.mapErr(…)</code>. Runtime faults and <code>unreachable</code> become outcomes of the run</td><td>Every failure is visible in a type, and wrapping needs no boilerplate</td></tr><tr><td>Records</td><td><strong>Nominal</strong>: a record type is its name, and a literal takes the type its context expects</td><td>Same-shaped types can&#39;t be mixed up, and methods, operators, schemas and error messages have a name to use</td></tr><tr><td>Record literals in lambdas</td><td><strong>A lambda&#39;s record literal takes its type from where the call&#39;s result goes</strong>, with no syntax at the literal: a call takes the type expected of it before its lambdas are checked, so <code>let rows: List(Row) = users |&gt; map(fn u: { … })</code> builds <code>Row</code>s, a later stage&#39;s lambda reads their fields, and a literal nested in one takes its field&#39;s type (<a href="/reference/language/values-and-types">section 5</a>, open question 25). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>Mapping records into other records is the commonest data program there is, and it failed with &quot;which record type?&quot; though the type was written on the <code>let</code>. Literals already take the type their context expects; a lambda&#39;s body is one more context, so nothing new is learned, and a type named at each literal would repeat what the <code>let</code> says</td></tr><tr><td>Variants and types</td><td><strong>Where a type goes, a name means a type, never a variant</strong>, except a variant that shares its one type&#39;s name, <code>type Id = | Id(String)</code>. A variant there is an error that names its type (<a href="/reference/language/values-and-types">section 5</a>). Decided 2026-10-12</td><td>A variant named <code>Int</code> or <code>Date</code> silently turned every <code>Int</code> in its module&#39;s signatures into the variant&#39;s type, and the errors pointed far from the cause; Eyelang&#39;s lexer in Eyelang had to call its token kinds <code>IntLiteral</code> and <code>DateLiteral</code>. Refusing such names would cost the most natural names for token kinds and JSON values, while types and values never meet in one position, so letting each position see its own kind of name costs nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Variants by the type expected</td><td><strong>A variant name written alone that several imported modules declare is the one whose type is expected where it is written</strong>: an argument, a typed <code>let</code>, a return, a record field, inside <code>Some</code>, the other side of <code>==</code>, or a later use in the same body. Only where nothing says which is it an error that asks for the module, <code>a.Low</code>. Patterns already chose by the matched value&#39;s type. Decided 2026-10-12</td><td><code>ai.Effort</code>&#39;s <code>Low</code> and <code>High</code> clashed with Notify&#39;s priorities, and the first fix renamed them <code>LowEffort</code> and <code>HighEffort</code>: any two modules with common words such as <code>High</code> couldn&#39;t be imported together without qualifying every use. The type already says which, as Swift&#39;s and OCaml&#39;s do; record literals take their type from context the same way (G.14). From the lab, 2026-10-12</td></tr><tr><td>Results</td><td><strong>A function without <code>-&gt;</code> returns <code>()</code> when its body is a block</strong>, and its last value is dropped; one written <code>= expr</code> returns the expression&#39;s type, inferred. <code>while true</code> without <code>break</code> has any type, since only <code>return</code> leaves it</td><td>Entity methods, jobs and helpers that do something need no <code>-&gt; ()</code>, and a loop that serves until it returns needs no <code>unreachable</code> after it. From the type checker: Notify&#39;s delivery loop</td></tr><tr><td>Effects</td><td><strong>Inferred inside, stated at the boundary</strong>: public functions list their effects with <code>uses</code> (none means pure); a callback&#39;s effects pass to the call that gives it; a function type kept in data states its effects</td><td>Permissions show up in the diff without annotating every helper, and higher-order functions work with any callback</td></tr><tr><td>Operators</td><td><strong>Traits</strong>: <code>+ - * / %</code> call <code>Add</code>, <code>Sub</code>, <code>Mul</code>, <code>Div</code> and <code>Rem</code>, which any type can implement once per right-hand type; no implicit conversions</td><td>Money, vectors and units get real operators, and the result type is always known from the operands</td></tr><tr><td>Arithmetic</td><td><strong>Total types</strong>: <code>Int</code> has arbitrary precision; <code>/</code> and <code>%</code> need a <code>NonZero</code> divisor; indexing returns <code>Option</code></td><td>Removes the runtime errors that &quot;Result only&quot; would otherwise need</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed-width integers</td><td><strong>No operators; each operation names its edge.</strong> <code>Byte</code>, <code>Int32</code>, <code>Int64</code>, <code>UInt32</code> and <code>UInt64</code> add with <code>wrappingAdd</code> or <code>checkedAdd</code> (an <code>Option</code>), and likewise subtract and multiply; bit operations are methods too (<code>and</code>, <code>xor</code>, <code>shiftLeft</code>, …). <code>T.from(n)</code> converts an <code>Int</code> and is an <code>Option</code>; <code>x.toInt()</code> always fits. A literal that doesn&#39;t fit its type is a compile error, and an ASCII character literal is a <code>Byte</code> where one is expected (<a href="/reference/language/values-and-types">section 5</a>)</td><td>Overflow is never silent and never a fault: the code says at each step whether it wants wrapping, as a hash does, or a check, as a length does, as Rust&#39;s <code>wrapping_add</code> and <code>checked_add</code> do. Byte-level code, such as a lexer over <code>Bytes</code>, matches <code>&#39;-&#39;</code> instead of <code>45</code></td></tr><tr><td>Consistency</td><td><strong>Atomic inside eyelang</strong>; keyed commands and <strong>reconcile</strong> at the boundary with other services. A <code>@reconcile</code> query returns an <code>Option</code> of the command&#39;s outcome: <code>None</code> means the command never arrived, and only then is it sent again, with the same key</td><td>One database makes real transactions cheap; outside services own their own state; the one case where resending is safe is decided by the owner, never guessed. From the lab: a lookup that can&#39;t say &quot;never arrived&quot; leaves a lost command uncertain for good</td></tr><tr><td>Values as JSON</td><td><strong>One JSON shape everywhere</strong>: records are objects, a variant is an object tagged with <code>&quot;kind&quot;</code>, a type of fieldless variants is a string, a wrapped value is the value, <code>None</code> is null (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>)</td><td>Requests, replies, recordings, model schemas and the generated TypeScript and Go types agree, and a tagged union reads naturally as a discriminated union in TypeScript</td></tr><tr><td>Contracts over HTTP</td><td><strong>Attributes map a contract to HTTP</strong>: <code>@http(&quot;POST /v1/send&quot;, status: {202: Pending})</code> on an operation, <code>@http(names: snake)</code> and <code>@auth(…)</code> on the contract, the key&#39;s header and body field on <code>@key</code>; addresses and credentials come from the environment</td><td>The real APIs the lab calls (Notify, Miniflux) are described without code; a request that never went out is retried, and only a lost reply is <code>Uncertain</code>. From the lab: Notify&#39;s 200 and 202 answers and its idempotency header</td></tr><tr><td>Serving contracts</td><td><strong>A program serves a contract with <code>impl Contract { … }</code></strong>: one function per operation, with the contract&#39;s types and defaults, run as one transaction; a command with <code>@key</code> takes the key last</td><td>A service written in Eyelang shares one contract with its callers, and a key can name an entity, so repeats need no dedupe table. From the lab: Notify, written in Eyelang</td></tr><tr><td>Serving over HTTP</td><td><strong><code>eye run</code> serves each contract a program serves on its <code>@port</code>, read the way a caller sends it</strong>: the <code>@http</code> route picks the operation, arguments and replies are in the one JSON shape, <code>@auth</code> is checked before any code runs (401), each request is one transaction, and a result has the status <code>status:</code> gives its variant, else 200. <code>NOTIFY_PORT</code> moves the port, <code>0</code> to any free one (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>)</td><td>Callers and servers agree because both read the same attributes, and the key, not a cache of replies, makes a repeat safe, as for any service. From the lab: Notify written in Eyelang must answer the lab&#39;s other programs</td></tr><tr><td>Ports and callers</td><td><strong><code>@port</code> is where a service listens, and <code>@allow</code> who may reach it, checked at compile time.</strong> A contract a program serves must declare <code>@port</code>; a contract without one is a service outside the network, reached at its configured URL, that any module may call. <code>@allow(m, …)</code> lists the modules that may call its operations (besides its own module, the modules that serve it, and tests); each name is a module the program can import; <code>@allow(nobody)</code> lets no module call it; <code>@allow</code> needs <code>@port</code> (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>)</td><td>The lab&#39;s own Notify has a port and four callers, which network policy can be written from, while Telegram, Miniflux and NetBird are outside services with no port of ours to guard: a rule that every called contract declares a port would have given them made-up ones. Callers write no port: a call goes to the URL the configuration gives, and the policy opens only the contract&#39;s port, so a call configured for another port reaches nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Fresh and destructive commands</td><td><strong><code>@fresh</code> adds a <code>basedOn: Version</code> parameter and the outcomes <code>Stale</code> and <code>Expired</code>; <code>@destructive</code> adds an <code>approval: Approval</code> parameter.</strong> Both come after the declared parameters, before <code>key</code>, and a served <code>impl</code> takes them too. <code>Stale</code> means the state changed since that version, <code>Expired</code> that what the command was based on is gone; <code>Expired</code> is the one <code>approval!</code> gives. A <code>Version</code> has <code>==</code> and no order; a snapshot&#39;s <code>version</code> is one. Over HTTP they travel as arguments, a reply of 409 is <code>Stale</code> and 410 <code>Expired</code>, and both outcomes are objects tagged with <code>&quot;kind&quot;</code> (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>)</td><td>The check-then-act race is the usual bug in programs that act on another service&#39;s state, and the fix (send the version you read, let the service refuse) is easy to forget, so the contract asks for it and the match must handle the refusal. Two outcomes because a caller does different things: read again after <code>Stale</code>, give up after <code>Expired</code>. Reusing <code>approval!</code>&#39;s <code>Expired</code> keeps one word for &quot;too late&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Network policy</td><td><strong><code>eye gen policy</code> writes Kubernetes <code>NetworkPolicy</code> objects from the contracts</strong>: for each contract with <code>@port</code>, the pods of the module that serves it (else of the contract&#39;s own module) accept TCP on that port only from the pods of the modules whose code calls it, among those <code>@allow</code> lets. Pods are labelled <code>eyelang.module: &lt;module&gt;</code>; the namespace is the <code>--namespace</code> flag (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>)</td><td>The homelab deploys to Kubernetes, and a plain <code>NetworkPolicy</code> needs no CNI extension. The checker already knows who calls what, so the policy admits the callers the code has, not every name <code>@allow</code> lists: a module listed but not yet calling stays out until it does. The namespace is a deployment&#39;s choice, not the program&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td>Generated code</td><td><strong><code>eye gen go</code> and <code>eye gen ts</code> write each contract module&#39;s types, a client and a server stub</strong>, from the rules the interpreter follows (<code>internal/wire</code>) and the defaults it computes. TypeScript&#39;s types are the JSON, a variant type a union on <code>&quot;kind&quot;</code>; Go&#39;s are structs and interfaces with JSON methods. Only operations with <code>@http</code> get a client method and a route. The outcomes a contract adds are values in TypeScript and errors in Go (<code>*Uncertain</code>, <code>ErrStale</code>, <code>ErrExpired</code>); a stub answers as <code>eye run</code> does, 500 for a failure (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>)</td><td>Notify in TypeScript and any service in Go build against the contract, so a change that breaks them fails their build. One set of rules read by the interpreter and both generators, checked by a round trip through <code>eye run</code>, keeps the three in step. Go code checks <code>err</code> after every call, so the outcomes it must handle are errors there; a TypeScript union handles them as values</td></tr><tr><td>Events</td><td><strong>Published when the transaction commits, and handled once per key.</strong> <code>@on(event(e))</code> starts one run per event, and <code>@key(field)</code> on the event makes a repeat start nothing; <code>next!</code> counts every event published since the run&#39;s previous step</td><td>A reply that comes quickly is never missed, and a message the bus delivers twice never starts a second run. From the lab: a button pressed seconds after its question, and Telegram updates delivered again after a restart</td></tr><tr><td>Events between programs</td><td><strong>A contract&#39;s events go from the program that serves it to the programs that consume them, as <code>POST /events/&lt;topic&gt;</code> with the event&#39;s value and the contract&#39;s <code>@auth</code>.</strong> A subscriber takes them on the ports it serves contracts on and on <code>EYE_EVENTS_PORT</code>, answers 202 once the event is in its journal, and takes no events of the contracts it serves. The publisher sends each event to the base URLs in <code>NOTIFY_EVENTS_URL</code>, from a delivery saved in the commit that publishes it, again after no answer or a 5xx until it is taken, in order per subscriber; a 4xx drops it with a log line. At least once, and once per <code>@key</code> (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>). Chosen by the owner on 2026-10-12 (#135)</td><td>Another service&#39;s events had no way into <code>eye run</code>, so the approver&#39;s button press never reached the approvals program. Served operations already go over HTTP on a port with <code>@auth</code>, so events reuse the same listener, the same secret and the same JSON shape, and need no broker to deploy. Saving the delivery with the commit and sending it again is the outbox pattern: nothing published is lost by a crash, and the subscriber&#39;s <code>@key</code> makes a second delivery start nothing. One rule for direction (only the serving program publishes a contract&#39;s events) means an event never comes back to where it started, and no outside caller can forge the events a program publishes itself</td></tr><tr><td>Queries</td><td><strong>Pipelines</strong> (<code>filter</code>, <code>map</code>, …) over lists and entity sets alike. Over an entity set they run in memory today; compiling them to SQL waits for several runners on one database (open question 28)</td><td>The same code as for lists</td></tr><tr><td>Reactions</td><td><strong>Views trigger jobs</strong>: <code>@on(enters(view))</code>, <code>@on(leaves(view))</code></td><td>Replaces most polling cron jobs</td></tr><tr><td>Evolution</td><td>Adding a field with a default is automatic; anything else raises the entity&#39;s <strong><code>@version</code></strong> by one and adds a <strong><code>migrate from N</code></strong> function, from a row as it was stored at version N to the state at N+1. Migrations chain, with no gaps; <code>eye run</code> refuses to start if a stored row has no path to the current version</td><td>Stored data outlives code, and the shape of old data needs no declaration kept around: a migration reads the old row by name</td></tr><tr><td>Mutation</td><td><strong>Mutable value semantics</strong>: <code>var</code> locals, <code>mut</code> parameters and <code>mut self</code> methods, field assignment and <code>+=</code>. Callers write <code>mut</code> too: <code>unify(mut subst, a, b)</code>. Values never alias; there are no references or pointers</td><td>Systems code reads naturally, every place a value can change is visible in the diff, and without aliasing there are no cycles, so reference counting stays enough</td></tr><tr><td>Generics and methods</td><td>Lower-case <strong>type variables</strong> (<code>List(a)</code>), requirements with <code>where a: Ord</code>, and methods on a type with <code>impl Type { … }</code></td><td>Generic code without new brackets; types get their own operations</td></tr><tr><td>Shared behaviour</td><td><strong>Traits</strong> with explicit <code>impl</code>; equality, ordering, printing, hashing, saving and schemas are derived for data types whose parts have them. An impl lives in the module of the trait or of the type (an <code>impl</code> of a contract, which serves it, may go in any module), and a type variable has only the traits its <code>where</code> gives it</td><td>One mechanism for generic code; a requirement written once is checked at every call, and two modules can&#39;t give one type two meanings of a trait</td></tr><tr><td>Declarations</td><td><strong>Typed attributes</strong> (<code>@on</code>, <code>@memory</code>, <code>@priority</code>, …) on jobs, entities and contracts. Each attribute is declared with where it goes and what it takes, and the checker holds every use to that: <code>@concurency isn&#39;t an attribute; did you mean @concurrency?</code></td><td>One general mechanism instead of special syntax per feature, and a program can&#39;t say &quot;one at a time&quot; and quietly get five. From the lab: a misspelt attribute was ignored, and <code>@concurrency(1)</code> wasn&#39;t enforced</td></tr><tr><td>Starting jobs</td><td><strong>A run can be started with a key, and starts once per key</strong>: <code>ring.start(&quot;kitchen&quot;, key: &quot;kitchen&quot;)</code>. <code>main</code> runs on every start of <code>eye run</code>, so a job it starts must have a key</td><td>The obvious way to start long-lived work from <code>main</code> is also the right one, and an event or command that comes twice starts one run. From the lab: a restart started the morning&#39;s job a second time</td></tr><tr><td>Effects at the boundary</td><td><strong>Public entity methods and public jobs state their effects as public functions do, and starting a job is an effect, <code>start J</code>.</strong> The key rule for <code>main</code> follows calls through it: a function or method <code>main</code> calls that starts a job without a key is refused. <code>eye fmt</code> writes the clauses (<a href="/reference/language/effects-and-signatures">section 12</a>)</td><td>A method that starts a job, or a job that reads files, does as much as a function that does, and a reviewer needs to see it in the same place. Starting a job is a change to the outside of the call: in a job a function that starts one is a recorded step, and a helper could otherwise hide from <code>main</code>&#39;s key rule a start that repeats on every restart. From the spec review of 2026-10-12</td></tr><tr><td>Reading entities in jobs</td><td><strong>A job reads stored state only in recorded steps</strong>: <code>w.read!()</code>, and <code>idleWorlds.read!()</code> or <code>World.all.read!()</code>, which give a list of snapshots. In a job or a waiting function, <code>w.phase</code>, a view and <code>World.all</code> read any other way are errors that name the step; outside jobs <code>w.phase</code> is short for <code>w.read().phase</code>, and a reference&#39;s identity reads anywhere (sections 14 and 15)</td><td>A resumed run must see the values it saw, and an unrecorded read in a replayed statement sees the entity as it is now. Making the reads errors, rather than quietly recording them, keeps every step visible as a <code>!</code>. From the spec review of 2026-10-12: a job read <code>w.phase</code>, slept, and recorded a value the test had changed meanwhile</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting functions</td><td><strong>A function whose body has <code>!</code> steps is a waiting function</strong>: it has <code>wait</code> in its effects, and jobs and other waiting functions call it with <code>!</code>, but <code>main</code> and tests can&#39;t. Its steps are the job&#39;s, so it follows a job&#39;s rules for them: every outside call is a <code>!</code> step in it, <code>env.get!(…)</code> included. Its frame is part of the run&#39;s saved state, so what can&#39;t be saved can&#39;t be live across a <code>!</code> in it either, its parameters included. <strong>Outside a job a wait blocks</strong>: <code>sleep(1 s)</code> in <code>main</code> holds it, and the call keeps <code>wait</code> in the caller&#39;s effects; a job can&#39;t call a function that waits that way (<a href="/reference/language/effects-and-signatures">section 12</a>)</td><td>Every wait in a run is a recorded step, so a job can&#39;t block on a sleep a helper hides. Outside jobs blocking is what a script expects, and <code>wait</code> in a signature shows that a call can hold its caller up. From the spec review of 2026-10-12: <code>pub fn pause() { sleep(1 s) }</code> passed as pure. The environment is input too: a proxy read without <code>!</code> in the lab&#39;s <code>web.page</code> chose the branch, and a replay after the variable changed would have taken another (an unrecorded mark for configuration was weighed and left out, since pinning runs to their code, open question 41, removes what it was for). A <code>Secret</code> parameter held across a <code>!</code> in a waiting function passed until 2026-10-12, though compiled jobs save that frame</td></tr><tr><td>Functions across a <code>!</code></td><td><strong>A function value is saved as the name of its code and what it captured</strong>, so it can be live across a <code>!</code> when the checker sees both: a lambda written in the job or waiting function whose captures can be saved, a function declared with <code>fn</code>, or a waiting function&#39;s parameter, whose callers give it one of those when it keeps the parameter across a <code>!</code>. A function from anywhere else, such as a field or another call&#39;s result, can&#39;t be. Functions still have no <code>Save</code>: they are never a job&#39;s argument, a step&#39;s result or an entity&#39;s state (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>)</td><td>A waiting helper that takes a callback, such as <code>retry!(3, fn: notify.send!(…))</code>, is ordinary code, not a built-in. Compiled frames already name a waiting lambda&#39;s code statically and save its captures; the same holds for any function a frame keeps. Function types don&#39;t say what a closure captured, so the check follows where the value came from instead of adding captures to types (open question 42, decided 2026-10-12)</td></tr><tr><td>Programs</td><td><strong><code>main</code> is an ordinary function</strong>; files, processes and other I/O are effects called directly. <code>!</code> marks durable steps, and is used only in jobs and in the waiting functions they call. <code>unreachable(&quot;why&quot;)</code> is the one way for code to stop itself</td><td>The core is complete without the durable runtime; jobs stay explicit about every step</td></tr><tr><td>What a fake received</td><td><strong><code>fakes(…)</code> is a value; <code>fake.calls(op)</code> lists the calls it answered</strong>, each a record of the call&#39;s arguments with defaults filled in, and the key (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>)</td><td>A test checks what a program sent to a service without the program keeping a record only tests read, such as the digest&#39;s <code>Day.sent</code>; a record of the arguments compares whole, and shows what differs when a test fails</td></tr><tr><td>Callbacks and stored functions</td><td><strong>A parameter&#39;s function type without <code>uses</code> takes a callback; every other function type states its effects.</strong> A callback may do anything, and its effects count where it is given. A lambda or named function kept in a field, a collection or a result does no more than that type states, and a callback can&#39;t be kept in data without its parameter&#39;s type stating its effects (<a href="/reference/language/effects-and-signatures">section 12</a>)</td><td>Higher-order functions stay generic over effects without effect variables, and a stored function can&#39;t do behind its caller&#39;s back what the caller&#39;s signature doesn&#39;t show</td></tr><tr><td>Callbacks read what can change</td><td><strong>A lambda given straight to a callback parameter may read a <code>var</code>, a <code>mut</code> parameter and <code>self</code> in a <code>mut self</code> method or an entity&#39;s state, as their values at the call, and can&#39;t assign them; any other lambda can&#39;t capture them.</strong> A lambda that reads a variable counts as reading it in the call, so the variable can&#39;t also be passed there as <code>mut</code>. Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12 for <code>var</code>s; <code>mut</code> parameters and <code>self</code> follow the same rule, which settles that a <code>mut self</code> method&#39;s lambdas could capture <code>self</code> anywhere while a <code>mut</code> parameter could be captured nowhere (<a href="/reference/language/mutation">section 7</a>)</td><td><code>items |&gt; filter(fn l: !(seen |&gt; contains(l)))</code> with <code>seen</code> a <code>var</code> is one of the commonest loops, and copying <code>seen</code> into a <code>let</code> first was ceremony. A callback can&#39;t outlive the call and values never alias, so it sees the one value the variable has during the call; a kept lambda could run after the variable changed, and would see an old value without saying so</td></tr><tr><td>Writing effects</td><td><strong><code>eye fmt</code> writes public functions&#39; <code>uses</code> clauses.</strong> Formatting a program&#39;s directory checks the program and writes each public function&#39;s inferred effects into its signature: it adds what is missing and drops what the body no longer does. A file formatted alone, and a program with other errors, keep their clauses as written (<a href="/reference/language/effects-and-signatures">section 12</a>)</td><td>Effects in a signature are there for the reviewer, who reads them in the diff; working them out and keeping them current is the compiler&#39;s job, not the author&#39;s, and a permission the code stopped using doesn&#39;t linger</td></tr><tr><td>Live tests</td><td><strong><code>eye test --live ai,notify</code> sends the named modules&#39; calls to the real services, and a test that can&#39;t reach one is skipped, not failed; <code>--record</code> writes their answers where the fakes read them</strong>, merged by operation and key, only from tests that passed, and never with a secret in them (<a href="/reference/language/time-secrets-tests">section 20</a>)</td><td>The same tests check the program against the recordings every day and against the real services when asked, with no second set of tests; a missing key on a laptop or in CI is a skip with a reason, and a re-recorded file is reviewed in the diff like code</td></tr><tr><td>Tests and the machine</td><td><strong>A test works in an empty directory of its own, and draws random numbers from a fixed seed.</strong> <code>fs</code> and <code>process</code> resolve relative paths there, and it is removed when the test ends</td><td>A test that touches files or runs programs gives the same result on every run and every machine, and can&#39;t change the repository it runs in</td></tr><tr><td>Concurrency</td><td><strong>Structured</strong>, without <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>: <code>parallelMap</code>, <code>both</code>, <code>race</code> start tasks that finish before the call returns, and a waiting task parks without blocking others. Tasks share only immutable values. From v0.2, task groups with <code>spawn</code> and bounded channels, also scoped</td><td>Parallelism without coloured functions or data races; back-pressure keeps memory bounded</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamic dispatch</td><td>Static by default; <strong><code>dyn Trait</code></strong> from v0.2 for values whose type is known only at run time (<code>fn notifier(config) -&gt; dyn Notifier</code>, <code>List(dyn Handler)</code>): a value converts to it where one is expected, and has only the trait&#39;s methods</td><td>The cost of a run-time lookup is visible in the type; libraries can accept implementations they don&#39;t know</td></tr><tr><td>AI</td><td><strong>Typed model calls and agents, as library code</strong>, with no syntax that serves only them: a call names the Eyelang type it wants, the compiler derives a JSON Schema from it, and the reply is checked and decoded into that type. Tools are functions whose effects become the caller&#39;s; in jobs every model call is a recorded <code>!</code> step; coding agents are sessions that durable jobs own</td><td>No new syntax: permissions, cost and nondeterminism are handled by effects, budgets and recordings, which already exist</td></tr><tr><td>Syntax</td><td><strong>Add syntax when it is generally useful and clearly better than a library function</strong>; syntax that also makes one area (jobs, agents) much better is welcome if it pays off elsewhere too</td><td>Keeps the language small without making common code clumsy</td></tr><tr><td>Interop</td><td><strong><code>extern fn</code></strong> for C functions and for functions provided by a Go program that embeds Eyelang</td><td>Reuse existing libraries; the standard library&#39;s primitives use the same mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>Keywords</td><td><strong>Only statement and expression words are reserved.</strong> Declaration words (<code>type</code>, <code>entity</code>, <code>state</code>, <code>job</code>, <code>view</code>, <code>test</code>, …) are keywords only where a declaration starts</td><td>Common names such as <code>state</code>, <code>view</code>, <code>type</code> and <code>test</code> stay usable</td></tr><tr><td>Blocks</td><td><strong>Braces</strong>, only where they hold several statements. A branch that is one expression or one assignment needs none: <code>if c then a else b</code>, <code>Pattern =&gt; value</code>, <code>Ok(t) =&gt; found += [t]</code>, <code>fn x: value</code>, <code>fn f() = value</code>. Layout is free; indentation means nothing</td><td>Keeps parsing simple and pasting safe, with no whitespace pitfalls, while one-liners stay light. From the lab: sorting results into lists in a <code>match</code> needed braces on every arm</td></tr><tr><td>Strings</td><td><strong>One line, or <code>&quot;&quot;&quot;</code> for many lines.</strong> A multi-line string starts with <code>&quot;&quot;&quot;</code> and a line break and ends with <code>&quot;&quot;&quot;</code> on a line of its own, apart from what follows it; that line&#39;s indentation is removed from every line (<a href="/reference/language/lexical-structure">section 4</a>)</td><td>Prompts and messages read as text in the code, an unclosed <code>&quot;</code> still can&#39;t swallow a file, and the formatter re-indents code without changing a string. From the lab: the digest&#39;s 40-line prompt</td></tr><tr><td>String positions</td><td><strong>A position in a string is an <code>Offset</code>, not an <code>Int</code></strong>, as Swift&#39;s <code>String.Index</code> is. <code>s.indexOf(part)</code> gives one, <code>s.slice(a..b)</code> takes a <code>Range(Offset)</code>, and <code>s.start()</code>, <code>s.end()</code>, <code>s.after(i)</code> and <code>s.before(i)</code> make and move them; <code>size</code>, <code>take</code> and <code>drop</code> count characters, in <code>Int</code>s</td><td>Scanning by position costs nothing per step and can&#39;t cut a character in two, and a count can&#39;t be passed where a position goes: with <code>Int</code> offsets, <code>s.slice(0..(s |&gt; size))</code> on <code>&quot;Málaga&quot;</code> quietly dropped the last letter. From the lab: the digest&#39;s HTML-to-text scanner</td></tr><tr><td>Arguments</td><td><strong><code>name:</code> alone passes the variable of that name</strong>: <code>send(key:)</code> is <code>send(key: key)</code></td><td>Keys, priorities and recipients pass through several layers under their own names, and the label still shows at each call. From the lab: <code>key: key</code> and <code>priority: priority</code> in every layer of the digest and the reminders</td></tr><tr><td>Statements</td><td><strong>A newline ends a statement</strong> unless the next line starts with a binary operator, <code>.</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>,</code>, <code>:</code>, <code>=</code>, <code>-&gt;</code> or <code>else</code> (<a href="/reference/language/lexical-structure">section 4</a>). <code>;</code> also separates statements</td><td>No semicolons, and a long pipeline can still break before each `</td></tr><tr><td>Braces in expressions</td><td>A <code>{</code> after <code>if … </code>, <code>else</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>let … else</code>, a signature, <code>test &quot;…&quot;</code> or a lambda&#39;s parameters opens a block; anywhere else it starts a record or map. After <code>=&gt;</code> it opens a block unless <code>name:</code>, <code>name,</code> or <code>name with</code> follows, which only a record can start. Variant payloads use parentheses: <code>Pending(receipt: id)</code></td><td>Every <code>{</code> has one reading, found with at most two tokens of lookahead, in both parsers; a match arm returns a record without extra parentheses</td></tr><tr><td>Pipes</td><td>Data-first: `x</td><td>&gt; f(a)<code>is</code>f(x, a)<code>; </code>_<code>places the value elsewhere:</code>x</td></tr><tr><td>Functions as methods</td><td><strong><code>x.f(a)</code> is <code>f(x, a)</code> when <code>f</code> is a function in scope whose first parameter takes <code>x</code></strong>, after the methods of <code>x</code>&#39;s type and a field of <code>x</code> that holds a function; a function imported by name counts, and otherwise the built-in of that name. The type must be known, a function that changes its first parameter is called <code>f(mut x)</code>, the editor offers these functions after <code>x.</code>, and <code>eye fmt</code> rewrites neither form (<a href="/reference/language/methods-and-generics">section 8</a>). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>The built-ins already worked as methods and the program&#39;s functions didn&#39;t, which gave the built-ins two spellings and the program&#39;s one. Pipes are rare in the lab&#39;s code, and methods read left to right without a `</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Reference counting with in-place reuse of uniquely owned values; immutable values; entities referenced by ID</td><td>No GC pauses, and no reference cycles by construction</td></tr><tr><td>Parsing</td><td>A <strong>tree-sitter grammar is the spec</strong>; the compiler&#39;s parser is hand-written; a conformance suite fails CI if the two disagree</td><td>One definition of the syntax, editor support for free, and compiler error messages under our control</td></tr><tr><td>Metaprogramming</td><td><strong>No user-defined macros before v1.0.</strong> <code>entity</code>, <code>job</code>, <code>contract</code> and <code>view</code> are built in, each lowered by its own compiler pass. Deriving is limited to the built-in traits; rare code generation goes through a build step (<code>eye generate</code>)</td><td>Fixed constructs give clearer errors and are easier for agents to work with; a macro system would delay v0.1; separate passes keep the option open</td></tr><tr><td>Program root</td><td><strong><code>eye.toml</code> marks the directory a program starts from</strong>; a file belongs to the nearest one above it, else to its own directory</td><td>Tools given one file, such as an editor jumping to a definition, must find the modules it imports: <code>lab/digest/digest.eye</code> imports <code>lab/home</code>. A marker file says so once, and it is the file that packages (open question 5) need anyway</td></tr><tr><td>Built-in docs</td><td><strong>The built-ins are declared in Eyelang</strong>, in <code>ir/builtin.eye</code>, with <code>extern type</code>, <code>extern fn</code> and <code>##</code> comments; the checker checks calls against those signatures and takes effects from their <code>uses</code>, and the interpreter implements them</td><td>Like Go&#39;s <code>builtin</code> package: an editor goes to a built-in&#39;s documented declaration as to any other, and the reference is written in one place, next to the signatures it describes. Since the checker uses them, the docs can&#39;t drift from the rules</td></tr><tr><td>Doc comments</td><td><strong><code>##</code> comments are Markdown, with a first-sentence summary, <code>[Name]</code> links that are checked, and a module doc at the top of a file</strong>. One extractor feeds <code>eye doc</code>, the editor&#39;s hover and completion, and AI schemas</td><td>One source of documentation for people, editors and models. Checked links keep docs from pointing at renamed code, and the summary rule gives every list a short line without a second comment</td></tr><tr><td>Answering approvals</td><td><strong>An answer names its request&#39;s <code>id</code>, and only the person in <code>from:</code> may give it.</strong> The runtime refuses any other answer and logs it. Text matches only from the command line and in tests, and is refused when it matches more than one waiting approval. An <code>Approval</code> has <code>what</code> and <code>by</code> fields (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>)</td><td>An <code>Approval</code> is the one value that stands for a person&#39;s consent, so the obvious routing job must be the safe one: one that forgets to check who pressed the button can&#39;t grant it, and an answer can&#39;t reach a request it wasn&#39;t meant for. From the lab: two runs asking &quot;Restart the router?&quot; were answered by one press (open question 17)</td></tr><tr><td>Recorded steps</td><td><strong><code>!</code> marks every recorded step in a job</strong>: every outside effect, every entity call, publishing an event (<code>bus.pinged.publish!(…)</code>) and starting a child run (<code>digest.start!(day)</code>). The effects of <a href="/reference/language/effects-and-signatures">section 12</a>&#39;s table are inferred, <code>publish C.event</code>, <code>consume C.event</code>, <code>secret &quot;name&quot;</code> and <code>ffi</code> included. A step&#39;s result is recorded, so it must be something that can be saved. Output is not a step: <code>print</code> keeps <code>stdio</code> for signatures, and logging is not an effect (<a href="/reference/language/effects-and-signatures">section 12</a>)</td><td>Every point where a run can pause or commit is visible, with no exception to remember; output is a side channel for people, and marking it would put <code>!</code> on every log line (open questions 19 and 22)</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign values</td><td><strong><code>extern type</code> is the one way to name a value foreign code owns</strong>; <code>Handle(name)</code> is gone. A program&#39;s <code>extern type</code> has no <code>Save</code>, <code>Show</code>, <code>Eq</code>, <code>Hash</code> or <code>Schema</code>, so it can&#39;t be saved, live across a <code>!</code>, printed, compared or asked of a model (<a href="/reference/language/calling-c-and-go">section 18</a>)</td><td>One obvious way, a name the checker tells apart from every other, and the saving rule applied to it: an <code>extern type</code> could be held across a <code>!</code> while a <code>Handle</code> couldn&#39;t (open question 21)</td></tr><tr><td>Loading C</td><td><strong>The interpreter loads <code>@library</code> functions with purego</strong> (github.com/ebitengine/purego), which calls the system&#39;s <code>dlopen</code> and then C functions from Go without cgo; the loader is <code>internal/ffi</code>, which the Go runner can also load compiled programs with. <strong>The released <code>eye</code> for Linux leaves the loader out</strong>, since it makes a binary dynamically linked; <code>go build -tags clib</code> puts it in, and on macOS and Windows it is always in (<a href="/reference/language/calling-c-and-go">section 18</a>). Decided 2026-10-12</td><td>Linux has no system call that loads a library: <code>dlopen</code> belongs to the C library, so the choice was a loader of our own, which would also have to start libc for each library it loads, or the system&#39;s. The lab runs <code>eye</code> on <code>scratch</code> images, which have no dynamic linker, so the release must stay static</td></tr><tr><td>Host functions</td><td><strong>A <code>@host</code> function is any Go function whose types match its declaration</strong>, checked when it is registered: <code>Int</code> is a Go <code>int</code>, <code>Result(T, String)</code> is <code>(T, error)</code>, and an <code>extern type</code> is any Go pointer, struct, map, channel, function or interface type (<a href="/reference/language/calling-c-and-go">section 18</a>). Decided 2026-10-12</td><td>The embedding program writes ordinary Go, and a mismatch is an error at <code>Register</code> that names the parameter, not a wrong value at a call. Go&#39;s <code>int</code> and <code>error</code> are what Go code already has</td></tr><tr><td>Long runs</td><td><strong>A run&#39;s saved state is its live variables, not its history.</strong> The interpreter keeps a checkpoint at each statement of a job&#39;s body and replays only the steps since (from 2026-10-11); compiled code saves frames at each <code>!</code> from v0.4. The language gets no <code>continue</code>-as-new</td><td>A run that never ends, such as the lab&#39;s train watcher, costs the same on its tenth day as on its first, with no new syntax (open question 9)</td></tr><tr><td>Journal syncs</td><td><strong>The journal syncs before the runner does anything outside, not after each commit</strong> (group commit): one fsync covers every commit since the last. Run records are JSON in the line, a trail that moved on is written as the steps it gained, and a journal written before 2026-10-12 is read as it is and written in the new form (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>)</td><td>Nothing outside can tell the difference, since a crash loses only commits no one saw, and <code>main</code> starting 5,000 runs went from 3.9 s to 0.36 s; a looping job&#39;s commit from 1.9 KB to 0.45 KB. Reading the old form keeps the deployed digest running across the upgrade, where refusing it would have meant <code>--fresh</code></td></tr><tr><td>Job code changes</td><td><strong>A paused run resumes only at a resume point that still exists with the same live variables</strong>: in the interpreter, the same statement of the job with the same typed code and as many variables. <code>eye run</code> refuses to start otherwise, as it does for entity rows it can&#39;t migrate, and names the job and its runs. Replaying the steps since a checkpoint compares each recorded step&#39;s call with the call it replays and faults the run on a difference</td><td>A code change under a paused run is an error with a message, not a wrong value found later; the old job keeps its name until its runs finish, and the new code takes a new one. Finishing old runs on old code is rejected, since entities migrate forward and old code would read new state (open question 4)</td></tr><tr><td>Typed choices</td><td><strong>A type whose variants have no fields gets <code>T.variants()</code> and <code>T.parse(s)</code>, derived</strong>: every variant in order, and <code>Some</code> of the variant named <code>s</code>, else <code>None</code> (<a href="/reference/language/traits">section 11</a>)</td><td>A person&#39;s choice, such as a button, is matched as a variant, so a misspelt or forgotten choice fails to compile, and the buttons come from the type the match uses. No syntax: they are calls on a type, as <code>Lexer.new</code> is (open question 15)</td></tr><tr><td>Module roots</td><td><strong><code>eye.toml</code> may list <code>modules</code>, more directories to find imports in</strong>, searched after the program&#39;s own directory, in order (<a href="/reference/language/programs-and-concurrency">section 13</a>)</td><td>A program deployed alone, such as the lab&#39;s digest, names the lab&#39;s modules in its <code>eye.toml</code> instead of reaching them through symbolic links that break and must list every module imported. From the lab: <code>deploy/digest</code></td></tr><tr><td>Native code</td><td><strong>Compiled programs are C, built by the <code>zig cc</code> that <code>eye</code> ships</strong>, with Perceus reference counting, whole-program specialisation and jobs resuming from saved frames (<a href="https://github.com/ruxxzebre/eyelang/blob/dev/docs/internals/compilation.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">docs/internals/compilation.md</a>). <code>eye</code> itself stays free of cgo: the interpreter loads C libraries at run time without it</td><td>The workload needs memory and resume time more than the best code, and C at <code>-O2</code> gives both; the bootstrap seed is C anyway; Koka and Lean 4 compile reference-counted functional code to C. Shipping <code>zig cc</code> cross-compiles to all five release targets from any host, with the same compiler everywhere</td></tr><tr><td>Generic private functions</td><td><strong>A private function that leaves its types out is generic</strong>: its body is checked on its own, and each call decides afresh what the body leaves open, so <code>fn twice(n) = n + n</code> works on <code>Int</code>s and <code>Float</code>s alike. A call that gives types without the operators or traits the body uses is an error at the call. Functions that call each other share one type while they are checked (<a href="/reference/language/bindings-functions-and-pipes">section 6</a>)</td><td>One type for the whole program made a helper&#39;s first caller decide it for every other, and the error appeared at the second caller, far from the cause. Let-polymorphism is what ML and Haskell do for the same reason, and compiled code specialises each use (section 22)</td></tr><tr><td>Schedules</td><td><strong><code>cron(&quot;…&quot;, in: zone)</code> takes the five standard fields</strong> (values, names, ranges, lists and steps; either day field matches when both are given), and no extensions. <strong>Daylight-saving changes are policies, <code>onGap: Shift | Skip</code> and <code>onOverlap: First | Second | Both</code></strong>, prelude variants, defaulting to <code>Shift</code> and <code>First</code> (<a href="/reference/language/time-secrets-tests">section 20</a>)</td><td>A crontab line copied from elsewhere means what it meant there, and its errors show at the string; the extensions (<code>L</code>, <code>W</code>, <code>#</code>, seconds) are rare and differ between crons. The defaults are what <code>daily</code> did before the policies could be chosen, and they need no hour check; <code>Both</code> is for jobs that should run every hour of real time. Variants, not strings, so a misspelt policy fails to compile</td></tr><tr><td>Joining runs</td><td><strong><code>allDone!</code> and <code>firstDone!</code> take a list of runs; <code>firstDone!</code> stops the runs that lost</strong>, which end as <code>Failed(Killed)</code> (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>)</td><td>A durable wait is a child run, so joining several needs nothing new beside <code>join!</code>, and each run&#39;s steps stay in one order. A loser that went on, such as an approval still waiting for an answer after its timeout, would act on a decision already made</td></tr><tr><td>Lock expiry</td><td><strong>A run still waiting inside <code>lock!</code> when the lock runs out ends as <code>Failed(LockExpired(name))</code></strong> (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>)</td><td>The expiry lets the next run take a lock a stuck holder keeps. Letting the old holder go on would run the guarded work twice at once, which is what the lock is for</td></tr><tr><td>Naming the joins</td><td><strong>The waits for several runs are <code>allDone!</code> and <code>firstDone!</code>, not <code>all!</code> and <code>first!</code></strong>; <code>all</code> and <code>first</code> are only the collection functions, and <code>all!(runs)</code> or <code>first!(runs)</code> is an error that names the wait to write (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td><code>!</code> only ever marks the same call as a step; it never changes which function a name means. With <code>all!</code> and <code>first!</code>, adding or dropping a <code>!</code> switched between a wait and a collection function, and a reader had to know both. From the spec review of 2026-10-12, design risk 11 (<a href="https://github.com/ruxxzebre/eyelang/blob/dev/docs/internals/reviews/spec-review-2026-10-12.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">docs/internals/reviews/spec-review-2026-10-12.md</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Ending entities</td><td><strong><code>end(ok)</code> in an entity&#39;s own method ends it for good</strong>: <code>ok</code> must be an <code>Approval</code>, the state is removed, and the store keeps only when it ended and whose approval let it. Calling or reading an ended entity faults; its identity never starts again. In an entity&#39;s method <code>end</code> is the entity&#39;s; elsewhere it is a string&#39;s end (<a href="/reference/language/entities">section 14</a>)</td><td>Ending deletes data, so it takes a person&#39;s consent that the compiler checks. If a later call started the identity again from its defaults, a job holding an old reference would bring back what a person approved ending, silently. Resolving <code>end</code> by context keeps <code>s.end()</code> and the spec&#39;s <code>end(ok)</code> both</td></tr><tr><td>Lazy iteration</td><td><strong><code>xs.iter()</code> gives an <code>Iter(a)</code>, a value that describes a walk and runs it again each time it is walked.</strong> Lists, sets, maps, <code>Int</code> ranges, strings and <code>Bytes</code> give one, and <code>s.chars()</code> is one; <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>take</code>, <code>drop</code>, <code>enumerate</code>, <code>zip</code> and <code>flatMap</code> on an <code>Iter</code> give an <code>Iter</code>, and the other collection functions walk it. Its functions only compute; it has none of the derived traits, so it can&#39;t be live across a <code>!</code>; and there are no endless sources (<a href="/reference/language/values-and-types">section 5</a>)</td><td>Walking twice gives the same elements because an <code>Iter</code> is a value, not a cursor a first walk would use up, which would be state shared by everyone holding it. Functions without effects make a second walk cost time and nothing else, wherever it happens. Sources that end keep <code>size</code>, <code>toList</code> and <code>sort</code> sure to finish</td></tr><tr><td>Division helpers</td><td><strong><code>/</code> and <code>%</code> keep their <code>NonZero</code> divisor, and <code>xs.average()</code>, <code>a.div(b)</code> and <code>a.rem(b)</code> give an <code>Option</code> instead</strong>: <code>None</code> for an empty list or a divisor of 0. <code>average</code> gives a <code>Float</code> for <code>Int</code>s too; <code>div</code> and <code>rem</code> round down as <code>/</code> and <code>%</code> do (<a href="/reference/language/errors-result-only">section 10</a>)</td><td>Every mean needed <code>match nonzero(xs |&gt; size) { … }</code>, so the obvious code was long. One result type for every list keeps <code>average</code> predictable, and a mean of <code>1</code> and <code>2</code> that is quietly <code>1</code> would be the wrong answer made easy; <code>xs.sum().div(xs.size())</code> is there for an <code>Int</code> mean (decided 2026-10-12)</td></tr><tr><td>Durations as numbers</td><td><strong><code>d.seconds()</code> and <code>d.millis()</code> give a <code>Duration</code> as a whole number, rounded down; <code>d / n</code> takes a <code>NonZero(Int)</code>, as an <code>Int</code>&#39;s <code>/</code> does; <code>d / e</code> of two <code>Duration</code>s is a <code>Float</code></strong> (<a href="/reference/language/time-secrets-tests">section 20</a>). Settled 2026-10-13, as issue #146 proposed</td><td>An average wait, the share of a lead time that has passed, and a timeout an API takes in milliseconds had no way to be written. Seconds and milliseconds are the units APIs take; a ratio of two durations has no unit, and rounding it to an <code>Int</code> would make <code>percent elapsed</code> quietly <code>0</code>. From the docs&#39; examples (log-summary, webhook-delivery)</td></tr><tr><td>Calls that come back</td><td><strong>A call tree is one transaction, and an entity has one state in it</strong>: a call that comes back to an entity whose method is still running starts from the tree&#39;s changes, the outer method goes on from the call&#39;s changes as from a <code>mut self</code> call, reads in the tree see the state in progress, and the entity commits once (from 2026-10-12, <a href="/reference/language/entities">section 14</a>)</td><td>Each call loaded its own copy and the last to finish won, so <code>A.ping</code> calling <code>B.pong</code> calling <code>A.bump</code> lost a write without a word. Refusing re-entry would be simpler, but it rejects programs that &quot;the whole call tree commits together&quot; invites</td></tr><tr><td>Model tools</td><td><strong>A function declared with <code>fn</code> and named where an <code>ai.Tool</code> is expected converts to one</strong>, as a value converts to <code>dyn Trait</code>; its parameters and result need <code>Schema</code>. Its effects count where it is named, and a model call&#39;s own effect is <code>net Ai.generate</code>, not <code>net</code>. In a job each model reply and each tool result is a step (<a href="/reference/language/ai-models-and-agents">section 19</a>)</td><td><code>tools: [searchIssues, label]</code> holds functions of different types, which no rule typed. A conversion keeps what a schema needs, the name, the <code>##</code> comment and the types, without a wrapper to write. A call whose own effect was <code>net</code> made every public caller list <code>net</code>, which covers any tool, so a new tool never showed in review. Recording each step keeps a restarted conversation from running a tool twice. From the spec review of 2026-10-12</td></tr><tr><td>Destructive tools</td><td><strong><code>@destructive</code> adds <code>approval: Approval</code> to a function as to a command, and a model&#39;s call of one is approved through <code>ai.generate</code>&#39;s <code>approve:</code></strong>, a function, usually <code>fn call: approval!(call, from: someone, within: …)</code>, called once for each call; without it the call is declined (<a href="/reference/language/ai-models-and-agents">section 19</a>)</td><td>One meaning for the attribute: what it marks runs only with a person&#39;s consent, which the type shows. &quot;Until someone approves&quot; named no one; asking through <code>approval!</code> answers whom with its <code>from:</code>, and keeps its rules (answers by <code>id</code>, only from the person asked) with no second way to ask. A function rather than <code>approver:</code> and <code>within:</code> arguments, which would copy <code>approval!</code>&#39;s. From the spec review of 2026-10-12</td></tr><tr><td><code>?</code> in a lambda</td><td><strong>A lambda&#39;s <code>?</code> returns from the lambda, so the lambda&#39;s result must be an <code>Option</code> for an <code>Option</code>&#39;s <code>None</code> and a <code>Result</code> for an error, which is wrapped into the lambda&#39;s error type.</strong> <code>?</code> on an <code>Option</code> in a function, job or lambda that doesn&#39;t return one is an error that names what it returns and points to <code>??</code> (<a href="/reference/language/errors-result-only">section 10</a>, from 2026-10-12)</td><td><code>return</code> can&#39;t leave a lambda, so <code>?</code> returning from the enclosing function would be the one way out, and the interpreter already returned from the lambda. Checking against the enclosing function let <code>xs |&gt; map(fn x: x? + 1)</code> put a <code>None</code> where an <code>Int</code> belongs, and <code>fn g(x: Option(Int)) -&gt; Int = x? + 1</code> returned a <code>None</code> from a function that returns <code>Int</code></td></tr><tr><td>Dropped results</td><td><strong>A statement can&#39;t drop a <code>Result</code> or a command&#39;s outcome</strong>, with or without the <code>Uncertain</code>, <code>Stale</code> and <code>Expired</code> a command adds: it must be used, matched, passed on with <code>?</code>, or discarded with <code>let _ = …</code>. A block&#39;s last expression is used when the block&#39;s value is; <code>()</code> and <code>Option</code>s may be dropped (sections 10 and 17). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>Dropping a failure without a word is the easy but wrong program: the tour&#39;s <code>notify.send!</code> lost <code>Refused</code> and <code>Uncertain</code>, and <code>fs.writeText(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;)</code> as a statement compiled. <code>let _ =</code> costs a few characters where a program means to ignore a failure, and shows that choice in review. An <code>Option</code> is a value that may be missing, not a failure, and is often dropped on purpose. From the spec review of 2026-10-12, finding 7 (<a href="https://github.com/ruxxzebre/eyelang/blob/dev/docs/internals/reviews/spec-review-2026-10-12.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">docs/internals/reviews/spec-review-2026-10-12.md</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Reading a run&#39;s outcome</td><td><strong>A test or <code>main</code> reads a run&#39;s outcome with <code>run.outcome()</code>, an <code>Option(Outcome(a))</code> that is <code>None</code> while the run is still going; only a job waits for one, with <code>run.join!()</code>.</strong> <code>run.join()</code> without <code>!</code> is an error that names the call to write, and <code>outcome()</code> in a job is an error that names <code>join!()</code> (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>). Decided 2026-10-12</td><td>A test can&#39;t wait, since its clock moves only when it calls <code>advance(…)</code>, but it must check how a run ended, including a <code>Failed(DeadlinePassed)</code> it expects. Before, <code>run.join()</code> in a test passed the checker and then failed at run time as the strings&#39; <code>join</code>, and tests wrote a job that joined and an entity that kept what it saw. <code>Option(Outcome(a))</code> rather than <code>Option(a)</code>, so a test sees a fault too. In a job a read that isn&#39;t a step could see another outcome on replay, so there it is <code>join!()</code>. From the ergonomics analysis of 2026-10-12</td></tr><tr><td>Calls without a contract</td><td><strong><code>http.get</code> and <code>ai.generate</code> try a service that can&#39;t be reached again for about a minute, as a contract&#39;s query does, and then return <code>Err</code></strong> with the reason in words, instead of ending the run as <code>Unavailable</code>: their results already say how they fail. <code>ai.generate</code> also tries again on 429 and 5xx answers. A request that reached the service and had no answer isn&#39;t sent again (sections 17 and 19). Decided 2026-10-12</td><td>Network rules in a new pod take effect seconds after it starts, and the digest&#39;s 07:40 run, missed while the pod was down, runs right then: contract calls waited for the network, and these two failed at once. They declare their failures, so a program that reads pages can report one it couldn&#39;t read and go on. Sending again only what never arrived keeps a slow page from holding a run for minutes, and a model call from being paid twice. From the lab, 2026-10-12</td></tr><tr><td>One retry rule</td><td><strong>The runtime tries again only where the program didn&#39;t ask to see the failure</strong>: an operation that declares <code>http.HttpError</code> gets it at once; one that declares no failures, and <code>http.get</code> and <code>ai.generate</code>, whose results always declare theirs, try again for about a minute what never reached the service (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>). Making declared-failure operations retry too was weighed and left: it would hide a minute inside each try of a program&#39;s own wait, and take away the only way to see a failure at once. Decided 2026-10-12</td><td>The owner asked for one rule. The digest polls Miniflux&#39;s <code>me</code>, declared <code>Result(User, http.HttpError)</code>, every minute for up to six hours; that program chose its wait, and the built-ins&#39; callers never could. From the lab, 2026-10-12</td></tr><tr><td>Commands that return a record</td><td><strong>A command may return a record as well as a variant type; its result is <code>Uncertain(key)</code> too, matched with an <code>Uncertain(_)</code> arm, and a field read before that is an error.</strong> Arms after an <code>Uncertain(_)</code> arm see the reply as declared (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>). Decided 2026-10-12</td><td>A service that answers with the thing it made, as Miniflux answers a new category, had to be wrapped in a one-variant type for <code>Uncertain</code> to be matched. The lost reply is the same either way; only reading a field of what may not be there needs stopping</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting runs leave RAM</td><td><strong>A run&#39;s record is in memory only while the run is ready.</strong> A run that waits or has ended keeps a handle in memory (its number, job, when it wakes, what wakes it, where it resumes); the journal and the Postgres store keep its record on disk and read it back when the run wakes or something asks for it, and opening a store reads no waiting run&#39;s record (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>). Decided 2026-10-12</td><td>Waiting that costs no memory is a promise (<a href="/design/goals">section 1</a>), and the records were what it cost: 20,000 runs sleeping for a day took 32 MB with their records in memory, and take 7 MB with their handles. A waiting run needs nothing of its record until it wakes; what the runner asks of it before then, when it wakes and what wakes it, fits in the handle</td></tr><tr><td>Element assignment</td><td><strong><code>xs[i] = v</code> and <code>xs[i] += v</code> change an element of a <code>List</code> in a <code>var</code> or a <code>mut</code> parameter, and <code>m[key] = v</code> inserts or replaces a <code>Map</code>&#39;s value; an index out of range is a fault</strong> (<a href="/reference/language/mutation">section 7</a>). Accepted by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>Changing one element was a rebuild, linear where every other language is constant, and the lexer&#39;s string stack paid it on every brace. It is local, visible mutation, as <code>p.x = v</code> is for records, and in place when the list is unique, so no principle moves. Reading stays an <code>Option</code>, since a missing element is a case code handles; writing where there is no element is a bug, like a negative shift, and a fault says so where a quiet no-op would lose the write. A map has no such edge: <code>=</code> is total there, and <code>m[key] += v</code>, which would need the missing key&#39;s value, is an error that names <code>m[key] = (m[key] ?? 0) + v</code>. From the benchmark review (<a href="https://github.com/ruxxzebre/eyelang/blob/dev/docs/internals/reviews/benchmarks-review-2026-10-12.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">docs/internals/reviews/benchmarks-review-2026-10-12.md</a>, <a href="/reference/language/lexical-structure">section 4</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Hex and binary literals</td><td><strong><code>0xC2</code> and <code>0b1010</code>, with <code>_</code> between digits, are integer literals of any integer type</strong>; the prefix is lower case, and <code>eye fmt</code> keeps the base and writes hex digits upper case (<a href="/reference/language/lexical-structure">section 4</a>). Accepted by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>The notation is universal, and byte-level code is unreadable in decimal: UTF-8&#39;s ranges were <code>194..=223</code> with <code>0xC2..=0xDF</code> in a comment. Keeping the base the author chose keeps a mask a mask; one case for the digits is the formatter&#39;s one style. A leading <code>0</code> stays decimal, so <code>007</code> is 7, not octal</td></tr><tr><td>String patterns over bytes</td><td><strong>Where <code>Bytes</code> or a <code>List(Byte)</code> are matched, a string literal is its bytes in UTF-8</strong>: alone it matches exactly them, and as an element of a list pattern it stands for them in a row, so <code>[&quot;..=&quot;, ..]</code> is a prefix (<a href="/reference/language/pattern-matching">section 9</a>). Accepted by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>A typing rule on syntax that exists, as a character literal is already a <code>Byte</code> where one is expected: <code>&quot;continue&quot;</code> instead of eight character literals, and no new prefix syntax. Exhaustiveness sees the list patterns they are</td></tr><tr><td>Bytes that aren&#39;t text</td><td><strong><code>fs.readBytes</code> and <code>fs.writeBytes</code> read and write a file&#39;s bytes, and <code>toBytes(xs)</code> makes <code>Bytes</code> of a <code>List(Byte)</code></strong> (sections 5 and 12). Accepted by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td><code>Bytes</code> came only from a string, so a lexer couldn&#39;t be given, or tested on, a file that isn&#39;t UTF-8. <code>toBytes</code> is named as <code>toSet</code>, <code>toList</code> and <code>toText</code> are; with element assignment on a <code>List(Byte)</code> it is how bytes are built one at a time, while <code>Bytes</code> themselves stay unchanged values whose slices share storage</td></tr><tr><td>A variant&#39;s number</td><td><strong><code>v.index()</code>, derived on a type whose variants have no fields, is the variant&#39;s place in <code>T.variants()</code>, counting from 0</strong> (<a href="/reference/language/traits">section 11</a>). Accepted by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>Go&#39;s <code>int(kind)</code>: a table, a checksum or a foreign format numbers a choice, and building a <code>Map(Kind, Int)</code> at the start to find out was ceremony. A derived method, like <code>variants</code> and <code>parse</code>, not syntax; <code>index</code> is the word the library already uses for a place (<code>indexOf</code>, <code>xs[i]</code>), and a type&#39;s own <code>index</code> method is called instead</td></tr><tr><td>Regular expressions</td><td><strong>A library module, not syntax: <code>regex.parse(pattern)</code> gives a <code>Result(Regex, RegexError)</code>, and patterns are RE2&#39;s, with no backreferences or lookaround. <code>re.matches(text)</code> asks about the whole text, <code>re.replace</code> replaces every match and <code>replaceFirst</code> the first, and a replacement names groups <code>$1</code> and <code>$&lt;name&gt;</code></strong> (<a href="/reference/language/values-and-types">section 5</a>). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>The digest read links out of HTML with a loop of offsets for what a pattern says in a line. RE2 matches in time linear in the text, so a match&#39;s cost is bounded whatever the pattern, as <a href="/design/goals">section 1</a> asks; backreferences would make it exponential. A function rather than a literal, since a library covers it and only checking at compile time is lost (open question 44). <code>matches</code> on the whole text is what checking an id or a date means; <code>replace</code> replaces every match as a string&#39;s <code>replace</code> replaces every occurrence, so changing a string to a pattern doesn&#39;t change how many are replaced. <code>$&lt;name&gt;</code> because <code>${name}</code> is interpolation in an Eyelang string. A <code>Regex</code> holds its pattern, so it has <code>Eq</code>, <code>Show</code> and <code>Save</code> as a record does, and a job can keep one</td></tr><tr><td>Schemas aren&#39;t recursive</td><td><strong>A type that holds itself, <code>()</code>, <code>Char</code>, <code>Byte</code>, <code>Bytes</code> and the fixed-width integers have no <code>Schema</code></strong>, so <code>eye check</code> refuses asking a model for them, at the call (<a href="/reference/language/ai-models-and-agents">section 19</a>); a tool that returns <code>()</code> answers <code>null</code>. Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>Anthropic&#39;s structured outputs take no recursive schema and OpenAI&#39;s strict mode limits them, so a <code>$ref</code> schema would work with one provider and not the other; the checker and the runtime now refuse the same types, where before the run faulted at the call. A tree is asked for as a flat list with parent names</td></tr><tr><td>Midnight is <code>00:00</code></td><td><strong>A time of day goes from <code>00:00</code> to <code>23:59:59</code>; <code>24:00</code> is not a time</strong>, and a literal that names no real date or time is an error at it (<a href="/reference/language/lexical-structure">section 4</a>). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>Each time of day has one spelling, so <code>daily(24:00, …)</code> can&#39;t mean two moments, and a schedule at <code>25:00</code> no longer fires at 01:00. The end of a day is the next day&#39;s <code>00:00</code></td></tr><tr><td>What recordings witness</td><td><strong>The recordings that count are those the tests replay with <code>fakes(m, from: &quot;…&quot;)</code>; a read is a field access or a pattern that looks inside a field; a variant matched without reading its fields needs no recording</strong> (<a href="/reference/language/contracts-talking-to-other-services">section 17</a>). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>The tests&#39; recordings are the ones checked against the contract and kept current, so they are the record of what the service sends; a stray JSON file isn&#39;t. Exhaustive matches must name every variant, so naming one can&#39;t need a witness, but using what it holds does. A contract with no recordings, or one the program serves, has no recordings to hold its reads to</td></tr><tr><td>Promises and principles</td><td><strong>Two lists, both in <a href="/design/goals">section 1</a>: five promises say what a user gets, and the principles say how we decide.</strong> The README and the docs site are written from the promises; CLAUDE.md points to the principles instead of keeping its own list, and its five pillars became promises (waiting, state, outside calls) or principles (values, not shared objects; visible where it matters). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-13</td><td>The main idea was written six times (the spec&#39;s goals, CLAUDE.md&#39;s pillars and its rules for deciding, the README, the site&#39;s home and why pages), and the copies had drifted: one said tests &quot;can&#39;t flake&quot;, one that every call is keyed, one that native compilation comes later. A list for readers and a list for deciding serve different people, and one place for each keeps them true</td></tr><tr><td>Tests don&#39;t reach the network</td><td><strong>In a test, a call to another service (a contract&#39;s operation, <code>http</code>, <code>ai</code>) is answered by <code>with fakes(…)</code> or goes out because <code>eye test --live</code> names its module; any other call fails the test at the call, with an error that names the <code>with fakes(…)</code> or <code>--live</code> to add</strong> (<a href="/reference/language/time-secrets-tests">section 20</a>). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-13</td><td>A test without fakes went to the service whenever its address or key was set, so it passed on one machine and failed on another, or sent a real message; &quot;tests replay recorded replies&quot; was a promise only <code>with fakes(…)</code> kept. Denying is the safe default; a general permission model for effects is open question 46</td></tr><tr><td>NaN equals itself</td><td><strong><code>==</code> on <code>Float</code>s asks whether two values are the same: <code>NaN == NaN</code> is true and <code>0.0 == -0.0</code> false, the one exception to IEEE 754. <code>Show</code> writes <code>NaN</code>, <code>+Inf</code> and <code>-Inf</code>, and JSON, which has none of them, refuses them: writing one is a fault</strong> (sections 10 and 17). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>Every value equals itself, so lists, sets, maps and derived record equality behave, and a test can <code>expect</code> a NaN; IEEE&#39;s <code>NaN != NaN</code> would make <code>[x] == [x]</code> false. <code>null</code>, which JavaScript writes, is <code>None</code>&#39;s shape, so <code>Some(nan)</code> would read back as <code>None</code>, and a service would read <code>null</code> for a number; a fault stops at the call that would send it</td></tr><tr><td>Memory in tests</td><td><strong><code>with memory(size)</code> in a test sets the memory its runner has, which <code>@memory</code> and <code>reserve!</code> are admitted against, for the rest of the test</strong> (<a href="/reference/language/time-secrets-tests">section 20</a>). Decided by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>Waiting for memory and <code>OutOfMemory</code> are outcomes a program plans for, so a test should show them deterministically, on any machine; a handler, as <code>clock(…)</code> is for time, rather than a flag to <code>eye test</code> that every test would share</td></tr><tr><td>Retrying a failed run</td><td><strong><code>eye runs --retry ID</code> starts a failed run again: it replays the steps it recorded and keeps its id, arguments and key; only <code>Unavailable</code> and <code>DeadlinePassed</code> are retried, and <code>--retry-failed</code> retries every run that ended so</strong> (<a href="/reference/language/jobs-and-waiting">section 15</a>). Accepted by the owner on 2026-10-12</td><td>A service that was down longer than its retries, or a run out of time, left a run failed for good, and only <code>eye run --fresh</code>, which throws away every entity and run, or a new key got past it. Replaying is what makes a retry safe: recorded calls aren&#39;t made again, and keyed and reconciled calls cover the one in flight. Keeping the id and key keeps <code>join!</code> and keyed starts pointing at the run. <code>Killed</code> was on purpose, and <code>Unreachable</code> is a bug that a retry would meet again</td></tr></tbody></table>',2)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
